IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mva-dates`
IMPORT `mva-core`

-- Motor Vehicles Act 1959 (SA), Part 4 -- THE INSURED PERSON'S SEAT: the owner or driver of a vehicle with a South
-- Australian compulsory third party (CTP) policy (or, for s 116(7)-(7e), the driver of an uninsured vehicle), against
-- whom a death or injury claim is made. Every question is asked as that person would ask it. The rules are in
-- mva-core.l4; this module restates none of them, and defines no name the claimant's seat (mva-seat.l4) defines.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website, as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025 (unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.

§ `The insured owner's or driver's questions`

§§ `Is my vehicle insured`

@export Was my vehicle covered by its South Australian CTP policy on the day of the accident, counting the 30-day period of grace after registration ends (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 99A(8), (10))?
GIVEN `the authority` IS A `The vehicle's authority` @desc What allowed your vehicle on the road: registration (trade plates count as registration), an exemption from registration, or a permit
      `the day it took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that registration, exemption or permit took effect
      `the last day of its period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period for which it was granted (the registration expiry date)
      `it was renewed or granted again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you renewed the registration, or obtained a new registration, exemption or permit, after that period ended
      `the day the renewal took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day the renewal or new grant took effect (any day if there was none)
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
GIVETH A `Policy in force answer`
`Was my vehicle insured on the day of the accident` MEANS
    `was a CTP policy in force (s 99A(8))` `the authority` `the day it took effect` `the last day of its period` `it was renewed or granted again` `the day the renewal took effect` `the accident day`

§§ `After an accident that killed or injured someone`

@export After an accident in which someone was killed or injured, have I met my duty to give the CTP insurer written notice, and what is my exposure if not (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `someone was killed or injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident, caused by or arising out of the use of the vehicle, killed or injured anyone
      `written notice was given as soon as practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether written notice of the accident, with the details s 124(1) lists, was given to the vehicle's CTP insurer as soon as practicable (by you or anyone else)
      `my defence` IS A `Accident notice defence` @desc Whether you did not know of the accident; gave the notice as soon as practicable after learning of it; believed on reasonable grounds it had been given; or, for a missing detail, made reasonable inquiries and complied to the best of your knowledge; or none of these
GIVETH A `Accident notice answer`
`Have I met my accident notice duty` MEANS
    `the accident notice duty (s 124(1)-(3))` `someone was killed or injured` `written notice was given as soon as practicable` `my defence`

@export What must my written notice of an injury accident to the CTP insurer state (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124(1))?
GIVETH A LIST OF `Accident notice detail`
`What must my accident notice state` MEANS `the details of the accident notice (s 124(1))`

DECLARE `Owner's or driver's duty` IS ONE OF
    `giving the insurer written notice of the accident (s 124(1), (2))`
    `co-operating fully with the insurer on a claim (s 124(3a), (3b))`
    `giving the insurer notice of a claim made on me, and the information it reasonably requires (s 124(4))`
    `producing my licence or the vehicle's documents when the insurer requires (s 124(5))`
    `not giving false or misleading notice or information (s 124(6), (6a))`

@export What is the largest fine I face for breaking this duty to the CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124)?
GIVEN `the duty` IS AN `Owner's or driver's duty` @desc Which duty: the accident notice; co-operating with the insurer (for the owner, including access to the vehicle); notifying a claim made on you; producing your licence or prescribed vehicle documents; or not giving false or misleading information
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the largest fine for this duty` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the duty`
    WHEN `giving the insurer written notice of the accident (s 124(1), (2))` THEN `the maximum fine for no accident notice (s 124(2))`
    WHEN `co-operating fully with the insurer on a claim (s 124(3a), (3b))` THEN `the maximum fine for not co-operating (s 124(3a))`
    WHEN `giving the insurer notice of a claim made on me, and the information it reasonably requires (s 124(4))` THEN `the maximum fine for not notifying the insurer of a claim (s 124(4))`
    WHEN `producing my licence or the vehicle's documents when the insurer requires (s 124(5))` THEN 750
    WHEN `not giving false or misleading notice or information (s 124(6), (6a))` THEN `the maximum fine for false information (s 124(6), (6a))`

@export What is the longest imprisonment I face for breaking this duty to the CTP insurer, in months (0 if the Act sets a fine only) (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124)?
GIVEN `the duty` IS AN `Owner's or driver's duty` @desc Which duty: the accident notice; co-operating with the insurer; notifying a claim made on you; producing your licence or prescribed vehicle documents; or not giving false or misleading information
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest imprisonment for this duty` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the duty`
    WHEN `giving the insurer written notice of the accident (s 124(1), (2))` THEN `the maximum imprisonment in months for no accident notice (s 124(2))`
    WHEN `not giving false or misleading notice or information (s 124(6), (6a))` THEN `the maximum imprisonment in years for false information (s 124(6), (6a))` TIMES 12
    OTHERWISE 0

@export A death or injury claim has been made against me: may I take this step without the CTP insurer's written consent (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126)?
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Insured person's step` @desc The step: starting or spending money on litigation; offering or promising a payment or settlement; making a payment or settlement; admitting liability; authorising repairs to, dismantling or wilfully damaging the vehicle; truthfully answering a police officer's reasonable question; or something else
      `the insurer has consented in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has given its written consent to the step
GIVETH A `Step answer`
`May I take this step without the insurer` MEANS
    `may the insured person take this step (s 126)` `the step` `the insurer has consented in writing`

§§ `What the insurer or the nominal defendant can recover from me`

@export Can the CTP insurer make me repay what it paid on a claim against me, for example because I was drink-driving, drove recklessly, failed to stop, or drove without the owner's consent (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 ss 123, 124A)?
GIVEN `I drove intending harm or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove (or did or omitted something with the vehicle) intending to cause death, injury or damage to another's property, or with reckless indifference to whether it would
      `I could not control the vehicle through alcohol or drugs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle (a court's finding on this is decisive)
      `my blood alcohol concentration` IS A NUMBER @desc Your blood alcohol concentration when driving, in grams per 100 millilitres of blood (for example 0.05; 0 if none)
      `I was found guilty under s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961 (the duty to stop and give assistance after a crash)
      `I broke another term of the policy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you broke any other term of the CTP policy
      `I failed to comply with s 124 or s 126` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to give the notices or co-operation s 124 requires, or took a step s 126 forbids without the insurer's consent
      `the insurer was prejudiced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that breach or failure (or the s 43 offence) prejudiced the insurer
      `I was in a road race` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you incurred the liability as a participant in a road race
      `I drove without the owner's consent and without lawful excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove the vehicle without first obtaining its owner's consent, and without lawful excuse
GIVETH A `Insurer recovery answer`
`Can the insurer make me repay` MEANS
    `what may the insurer recover from the insured person (ss 123, 124A)`
        (`Insured's conduct` WITH
            `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference` IS `I drove intending harm or recklessly`
            `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`         IS `I could not control the vehicle through alcohol or drugs`
            `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres`                       IS `my blood alcohol concentration`
            `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`        IS `I was found guilty under s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`
            `I broke another term of the policy`                                                 IS `I broke another term of the policy`
            `I failed to comply with s 124 or s 126`                                             IS `I failed to comply with s 124 or s 126`
            `the insurer was prejudiced`                                                          IS `the insurer was prejudiced`
            `I was a participant in a road race`                                                  IS `I was in a road race`
            `I drove without the owner's consent and without lawful excuse`                      IS `I drove without the owner's consent and without lawful excuse`)

DECLARE `Excess answer` IS ONE OF
    `no excess: the accident was not more than 25% your fault (s 124AB(1))`
    `no excess: the insurer has used another right of recovery against you (s 124AB(2))`
    `you paid within 1 month of the first request, so the excess is 95% (s 124AB(1)(a)(ii), (b)(ii))` HAS `the amount` IS A NUMBER
    `the full excess is a debt you owe (s 124AB(1)(a)(i), (b)(i))` HAS `the amount` IS A NUMBER
    `not yet paid: pay 95% by the last day, or the full excess after it` HAS `the reduced amount` IS A NUMBER, `the full amount` IS A NUMBER, `the last day` IS A DATE
    `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

@export How much excess can the CTP insurer recover from me for a claim it paid, and do I get the 95% rate for paying within a month (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124AB)?
GIVEN `my share of the fault` IS A NUMBER @desc Your share of the fault for the accident, as a percentage (for example 30 for 30%)
      `what the insurer paid and spent` IS A NUMBER @desc The money the insurer paid and the costs it incurred on the claim, in dollars
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `the CPI for the September quarter before the accident year` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the year of the accident
      `the CPI for the September quarter 2010` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2010
      `the insurer used another right of recovery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has exercised any other right of recovery against you under Part 4 (for example under s 124A)
      `the day of the first request` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer first requested payment of the excess
      `I have paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the amount requested
      `the day I paid` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your payment (any day if you have not paid)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Excess answer`
`How much excess can the insurer recover` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF `my share of the fault` AT MOST `the fault share that must be exceeded (s 124AB(1))`
         THEN `no excess: the accident was not more than 25% your fault (s 124AB(1))`
         ELSE IF `the insurer used another right of recovery`
              THEN `no excess: the insurer has used another right of recovery against you (s 124AB(2))`
              ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome`
                   WHEN `the duty was kept in time` THEN `you paid within 1 month of the first request, so the excess is 95% (s 124AB(1)(a)(ii), (b)(ii))` `the reduced excess`
                   WHEN `the time has not yet run out` THEN `not yet paid: pay 95% by the last day, or the full excess after it` `the reduced excess` `the full amount of the excess` `the last day to pay`
                   WHEN `the duty was not kept in time` THEN `the full excess is a debt you owe (s 124AB(1)(a)(i), (b)(i))` `the full amount of the excess`
    WHERE
        `the full amount of the excess` MEANS `the full excess (s 124AB(1))` `what the insurer paid and spent` (`the prescribed amount for the year of the accident (s 124AB(3)-(5))` (DATE_YEAR `the accident day`) `the CPI for the September quarter before the accident year` `the CPI for the September quarter 2010`)
        `the reduced excess` MEANS `the full amount of the excess` TIMES `the share payable if paid within 1 month (s 124AB(1)(a)(ii), (b)(ii))`
        `the last day to pay` MEANS `the day of the first request` `plus months` `the months to pay for the reduced amount (s 124AB(1))`
        `the outcome` MEANS
            `what became of` (`s 124AB(1) -- the insured person may pay 95% of the excess within 1 month after the first request, otherwise the full excess` (`the days from` `the day of the first request` `to` `the last day to pay`)) `for` `the insured person` `doing` `pay the excess the insurer requests` `done` `I have paid` `at` (`the days from` `the day of the first request` `to` `the day I paid`) `now` (`the days from` `the day of the first request` `to` `today`)

@export I was driving an uninsured vehicle: can the nominal defendant make me repay what it paid on the injury claim (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 116(7)-(7e))?
GIVEN `I was liable for the death or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or partly liable for the death or injury
      `I drove intending harm or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove intending to cause death, injury or damage to another's property, or with reckless indifference to whether it would
      `I could not control the vehicle through alcohol or drugs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
      `my blood alcohol concentration` IS A NUMBER @desc Your blood alcohol concentration when driving, in grams per 100 millilitres of blood (0 if none)
      `I was found guilty under s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been found guilty of failing to stop and give assistance (s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961)
      `the owner consented to the use` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was being used by, or with the consent of, its owner
      `I did not know and could not reasonably have known it was uninsured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not know, and could not reasonably be expected to have known, that the vehicle was uninsured
      `I was unlicensed for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you drove while not duly licensed or otherwise permitted by law to drive that vehicle
      `the vehicle was overloaded or unsafe` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was overloaded, or in an unsafe, unroadworthy or damaged condition
GIVETH A `Nominal defendant recovery answer`
`Can the nominal defendant make me repay` MEANS
    `what may the nominal defendant recover from the uninsured driver (s 116(7)-(7e))`
        (`Uninsured driver's facts` WITH
            `I was wholly or partly liable for the death or injury`                                IS `I was liable for the death or injury`
            `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference`  IS `I drove intending harm or recklessly`
            `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`         IS `I could not control the vehicle through alcohol or drugs`
            `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres`                       IS `my blood alcohol concentration`
            `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`        IS `I was found guilty under s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`
            `the vehicle was used by or with the owner's consent`                                 IS `the owner consented to the use`
            `I did not know and could not reasonably have known it was uninsured`                IS `I did not know and could not reasonably have known it was uninsured`
            `I was not duly licensed or permitted to drive it`                                    IS `I was unlicensed for it`
            `the vehicle was overloaded, unsafe, unroadworthy or damaged`                        IS `the vehicle was overloaded or unsafe`)

DECLARE `Indemnity answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the CTP policy insures you against that liability (s 104(1)(a))`
    `no: you are not entitled to be indemnified by the insurer for aggravated, exemplary or punitive damages (s 113A)`

@export Damages were awarded against me for a death or injury from my vehicle: will the CTP insurer cover this kind of damages for me (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 113A)?
GIVEN `the kind of damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc Compensatory damages for the death or bodily injury, aggravated damages, or exemplary or punitive damages
GIVETH A `Indemnity answer`
`Will the insurer cover these damages for me` MEANS
    IF `the insurer is barred from paying it (s 113A)` `the kind of damages`
    THEN `no: you are not entitled to be indemnified by the insurer for aggravated, exemplary or punitive damages (s 113A)`
    ELSE `yes: the CTP policy insures you against that liability (s 104(1)(a))`
